{"data":{"text":"The problem was a Rust macro scoping detail I mis-handled. Here's what went wrong:\n\n`#[macro_export]` macros are **exported at the crate root**, not inside the module where they're defined. In `ui.rs` I used `#[macro_export]` on `print_coloured!` and `println_coloured!`, so they landed at the crate root (`crate::print_coloured!` / `crate::println_coloured!`) — *not* at `crate::ui::print_coloured!`.\n\nBut in `main.rs` I wrote:\n\n```rust\nuse crate::ui::{println_coloured, print_coloured};\n```\n\nwhich looks for them under `ui` — where they don't exist. The compiler's own error message spelled this out and even suggested the fix (`use crate::{println_coloured, ...}`).\n\n**The fix I applied** removes `#[macro_export]` and instead leaves the `macro_rules!` definitions inside `ui.rs` with:\n\n```rust\npub(crate) use print_coloured;\npub(crate) use println_coloured;\n```\n\nThis makes the macros available at `crate::ui::print_coloured!` / `crate::ui::println_coloured!`, matching the existing `use crate::ui::{...}` import in `main.rs` — and keeps them `pub(crate)` so they're only visible within this crate.\n\nSo the working-tree diff now has:\n- `src/ui.rs` — the colour module (macros fixed to stay inside `ui`)\n- `src/main.rs` — the `mod ui;` declaration and the `use crate::ui::{...}` import\n\nHave you committed the fix yet, or shall I run `cargo check` to confirm and then commit?","tool_calls":3},"kind":"turn_complete","ts":"2026-08-27T01:52:24.967832736Z","turn":1}
